186
00:12:57,027 --> 00:13:03,849
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I meant, like, you replace the grief of the household life with the grief of renunciation, of a life of a monk.

187
00:13:03,869 --> 00:13:07,130
[SPEAKER_01]: You replace the joys of household life with the joys of renunciation.

188
00:13:08,270 --> 00:13:14,491
[SPEAKER_01]: You see it as greater, structurally, fundamentally, phenomenologically greater as a phenomenon.

189
00:13:14,932 --> 00:13:15,972
[SPEAKER_01]: It's more fundamental.

190
00:13:17,012 --> 00:13:22,613
[SPEAKER_01]: And same, you will be abiding in a noble silence simply by stopping mid-sentence, if you want.

191
00:13:22,914 --> 00:13:24,574
[SPEAKER_01]: You could establish your mind to that point.

192
00:13:25,360 --> 00:13:27,202
[SPEAKER_01]: Whereby, yeah, you're not in silence when you're talking.

193
00:13:27,222 --> 00:13:29,383
[SPEAKER_01]: The moment you stop talking, the mind goes back into silence.

194
00:13:29,844 --> 00:13:30,824
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it never left.

195
00:13:32,125 --> 00:13:34,507
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't do that if you never practice silence.

196
00:13:34,688 --> 00:13:36,609
[SPEAKER_01]: If you never practice restraint of your speech.

197
00:13:37,270 --> 00:13:40,833
[SPEAKER_01]: If you've just been chatting and talking constantly whenever there's an impulse to do so.

198
00:13:41,453 --> 00:13:46,477
[SPEAKER_01]: But obviously, you certainly won't do that either if you just, for the rest of your life, refuse to utter a word.

199
00:13:47,118 --> 00:13:50,360
[SPEAKER_01]: That won't necessarily reveal you the principle between speaking and non-speaking.

200
00:13:51,401 --> 00:13:52,522
[SPEAKER_01]: The verb of determination.

201
00:13:54,040 --> 00:13:57,201
[SPEAKER_01]: It's you trying to discern the principle, being pointed at it.

202
00:13:57,981 --> 00:13:59,421
[SPEAKER_01]: That might make you help discern it.

203
00:14:00,381 --> 00:14:08,123
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why you can't put the proper practice of dhamma that results in wisdom in any method or technique, because it's not about what you do.

204
00:14:08,763 --> 00:14:11,383
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about the recognition between things that you do and you don't do.

205
00:14:12,643 --> 00:14:14,684
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's neither where you do it nor where you don't do.

206
00:14:15,204 --> 00:14:21,225
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't pin down the practice in a mere mechanical behavior or repetition or avoidance of certain things.

207
00:14:22,080 --> 00:14:25,641
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, avoidance of unwholesome is necessary as a basis, but that's not a dhamma.

208
00:14:25,721 --> 00:14:32,582
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha was quite clear when he said that dhamma is not vinaya.

209
00:14:33,862 --> 00:14:34,903
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not vinaya.

210
00:14:35,183 --> 00:14:35,883
[SPEAKER_01]: It's as clear as that.

211
00:14:35,903 --> 00:14:38,063
[SPEAKER_01]: Because many people, many monks can't distinguish that.

212
00:14:39,224 --> 00:14:40,864
[SPEAKER_01]: They think if there's no vinaya, there's no dhamma.

213
00:14:42,224 --> 00:14:43,985
[SPEAKER_01]: Dhamma is not vinaya.

214
00:14:44,925 --> 00:14:45,945
[SPEAKER_01]: A very clear statement.

215
00:14:46,785 --> 00:14:51,106
[SPEAKER_01]: Followed by, but there is no practice of dhamma without presence of vinaya.

216
00:14:52,933 --> 00:14:54,014
[SPEAKER_01]: Without presence of urge.

217
00:14:54,854 --> 00:14:55,454
[SPEAKER_01]: So, aha!

218
00:14:56,175 --> 00:14:57,676
[SPEAKER_01]: There is something to be understood there.

219
00:14:57,996 --> 00:15:00,297
[SPEAKER_01]: If you understand that, you will be free from sīla dharmās.

220
00:15:00,437 --> 00:15:01,358
[SPEAKER_01]: You truly understand it.

221
00:15:04,099 --> 00:15:06,021
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is basically what I'm expounding on here.

222
00:15:06,361 --> 00:15:07,401
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I'm trying to explain.

223
00:15:08,602 --> 00:15:12,444
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your non-sensual behavior needs to become established.

224
00:15:13,085 --> 00:15:14,245
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to actually do it.

225
00:15:16,627 --> 00:15:20,829
[SPEAKER_01]: Because by doing a non-sensual behavior, you can't at the same time do the sensual behavior.

226
00:15:22,894 --> 00:15:35,477
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, yeah, you can spend the rest of your life neurotic and focused about your rules and virtue and never discern the principle in regard to, hold on, it's not about me doing the virtue.

227
00:15:36,278 --> 00:15:39,599
[SPEAKER_01]: My virtue is actually defined by not doing the non-virtue.

228
00:15:42,119 --> 00:15:43,780
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's a completely different thing.

229
00:15:48,661 --> 00:15:51,102
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why you can't put the practice into certain

230
00:15:52,252 --> 00:15:55,213
[SPEAKER_01]: A meditation, or just watch that, to do this, or walk like this.

231
00:15:55,593 --> 00:15:55,773
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

232
00:15:57,053 --> 00:15:57,873
[SPEAKER_01]: It makes no sense.

233
00:15:58,674 --> 00:15:58,974
[SPEAKER_01]: At all.

234
00:15:59,174 --> 00:16:00,114
[SPEAKER_01]: If it comes down to...

235
00:16:00,374 --> 00:16:04,395
[SPEAKER_01]: If you recognize that, actually, what I'm supposed to do is discern the principle between wholesome and unwholesome.

236
00:16:04,855 --> 00:16:13,977
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha would, like, to Mahāpajāpati, when she asked him to go on a retreat and give her a brief instruction, all he said was, whatever you know is unwholesome, abandon it.

237
00:16:14,438 --> 00:16:17,578
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever you know is wholesome, cultivate it.

238
00:16:17,598 --> 00:16:19,819
[SPEAKER_01]: So, she went in the mountains based on that.

239
00:16:23,163 --> 00:16:34,491
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can see that cultivating unwholesome... sorry, cultivating wholesome means basically cultivate, I mean, discern, dwell on it, bother on it, bring it into being until it's understood.

240
00:16:34,951 --> 00:16:41,175
[SPEAKER_01]: Because she wasn't in the mountain and doing charity work or some other wholesome things that people usually regard as good.

241
00:16:42,236 --> 00:16:43,537
[SPEAKER_01]: No, she was completely secluded.

242
00:16:44,458 --> 00:16:48,821
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when it's said to be cultivating wholesome, it means, yeah, you don't do unwholesome,

243
00:16:49,652 --> 00:16:53,475
[SPEAKER_01]: You're already calculating wholesome, when you know that that's what you're doing.

244
00:16:54,857 --> 00:17:07,628
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're unaware of the principle that by not doing unwholesome, you're automatically wholesome, so you don't have to then do wholesome, if you don't discern that principle, you remain bound by wholesome.

245
00:17:08,008 --> 00:17:09,950
[SPEAKER_01]: Consequently, you remain bound by unwholesome.

246
00:17:10,624 --> 00:17:20,933
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why sheer adherence to the rules and virtue can result in good rebirth, because you've been doing good actions, but it cannot take you outside of the sphere of kamma, sphere of action.

247
00:17:21,533 --> 00:17:28,219
[SPEAKER_01]: That's only reserved for those with the right view, which is where the dhamma begins, where the right view begins.

248
00:17:32,243 --> 00:17:37,748
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you was in a relationship... that's why I'm so insistent when people come and ask me questions that... that they don't...

249
00:17:38,609 --> 00:17:41,530
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't focus, don't think you can put it in a technique or something.

250
00:17:42,090 --> 00:17:44,431
[SPEAKER_01]: And they say, oh, I find it helpful or something.

251
00:17:44,491 --> 00:17:44,932
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not.

252
00:17:45,872 --> 00:17:50,374
[SPEAKER_01]: Your criteria of what's helpful, it's not right, or it's not existent very often.

253
00:17:50,854 --> 00:17:53,215
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what's helpful is just what feels good for most people.

254
00:17:54,916 --> 00:17:57,897
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you adhere to something, you think, this is what I should be doing.

255
00:17:58,357 --> 00:18:03,119
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, that's definition of sīla bhārata-pāramāsa, virtue and duty.

256
00:18:03,980 --> 00:18:05,400
[SPEAKER_01]: I need to be doing this.

257
00:18:05,920 --> 00:18:07,081
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's a first factor.

258
00:18:08,390 --> 00:18:11,491
[SPEAKER_01]: A first factor that prevents you from becoming another.

259
00:18:12,452 --> 00:18:28,698
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you... you then... well, if a person has some transparency, they will think, okay, so, I can either adhere to this neurotically for the rest of my life and do this... do this technique and whatnot, and remain a Bhutujana, or I can really figure out what it meant by that.

260
00:18:28,758 --> 00:18:31,019
[SPEAKER_01]: Because these are the words of the Buddha.

261
00:18:31,760 --> 00:18:36,722
[SPEAKER_01]: The Sīlabhata Paramahansa, it's basically a definition of virtue and duty.

262
00:18:36,742 --> 00:18:37,542
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a first factor.

263
00:18:38,512 --> 00:18:41,393
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the object of a composure?

264
00:18:41,413 --> 00:18:44,813
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you know what the object is?

265
00:19:00,959 --> 00:19:01,560
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

266
00:19:01,620 --> 00:19:03,742
[SPEAKER_00]: As opposed to the unwholesome I'm not doing.

267
00:19:03,822 --> 00:19:04,182
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

268
00:19:04,242 --> 00:19:07,225
[SPEAKER_01]: So that would be the... Yeah, because you can call that object as well.

269
00:19:07,245 --> 00:19:09,547
[SPEAKER_01]: But most people... What is an object?

270
00:19:09,567 --> 00:19:10,268
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I don't know.

271
00:19:10,868 --> 00:19:14,351
[SPEAKER_01]: A smiley face, a clay pot, a buddha rūpa or something.

272
00:19:14,572 --> 00:19:14,952
[SPEAKER_01]: An object.

273
00:19:14,972 --> 00:19:15,813
[SPEAKER_01]: Give me an object.

274
00:19:16,493 --> 00:19:17,514
[SPEAKER_01]: Give me something palpable.

275
00:19:17,574 --> 00:19:17,775
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

276
00:19:18,235 --> 00:19:23,640
[SPEAKER_01]: A recognition of unwholesome, if that's your present thought, is the object that you can focus on.

277
00:19:24,205 --> 00:19:31,111
[SPEAKER_01]: As a result of it, you will discern the relationship between the wholesome, that's automatically being defined, simply by not engaging in unwholesome.

278
00:19:32,171 --> 00:19:44,701
[SPEAKER_00]: So, another thing I was thinking about, then, in terms of... if one sees that, again, I'm not engaging in unwholesome acts, therefore I dwell in wholesomeness.

279
00:19:48,924 --> 00:19:49,265
[SPEAKER_00]: Quite.

280
00:19:50,326 --> 00:19:53,368
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a peaceful state, afflictive, but not active.

281
00:19:55,707 --> 00:20:02,453
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, well, one is determined by the other.

282
00:20:08,919 --> 00:20:11,361
[SPEAKER_00]: But then it says that neither are mine.

283
00:20:12,322 --> 00:20:14,364
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

284
00:20:18,568 --> 00:20:20,830
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I think, what is higher then?

285
00:20:21,447 --> 00:20:23,167
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the higher perception then?

286
00:20:23,627 --> 00:20:24,568
[SPEAKER_01]: It's what I'm talking about.

287
00:20:24,628 --> 00:20:26,548
[SPEAKER_01]: It's going beyond good and evil, good and bad.

288
00:20:27,048 --> 00:20:30,189
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the higher perception is self?

289
00:20:31,629 --> 00:20:32,069
[SPEAKER_00]: Not self?

290
00:20:33,029 --> 00:20:33,289
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

291
00:20:33,489 --> 00:20:34,029
[SPEAKER_01]: Fundamentally.

292
00:20:34,449 --> 00:20:36,410
[SPEAKER_01]: But that is already reserved only for the right view.

293
00:20:37,750 --> 00:20:38,190
[SPEAKER_01]: Not self.

294
00:20:38,710 --> 00:20:48,052
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, putusas can have higher perceptions in regard to sensuality, in regard to virtue and duties, all the upādānas that they can free themselves from, in regard to views,

295
00:20:48,531 --> 00:20:52,333
[SPEAKER_01]: But they can't have a high perception in regard to self and not-self.

296
00:20:53,074 --> 00:20:54,715
[SPEAKER_01]: If they do so, they'll become a Sotāpanna.

297
00:20:56,876 --> 00:20:57,677
[SPEAKER_01]: But it is exactly.

298
00:20:57,697 --> 00:20:58,617
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I'm talking about.

299
00:20:58,637 --> 00:21:04,521
[SPEAKER_01]: The discernment of the relationship of wholesome and unwholesome takes you above wholesome and unwholesome.

300
00:21:06,022 --> 00:21:07,623
[SPEAKER_01]: It takes you above things that you were doing.

301
00:21:08,624 --> 00:21:09,725
[SPEAKER_01]: It takes you above action.

302
00:21:11,966 --> 00:21:13,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means it cannot be appropriate.

303
00:21:13,667 --> 00:21:16,429
[SPEAKER_01]: Because only things that you can do are appropriate.

304
00:21:17,278 --> 00:21:21,602
[SPEAKER_01]: Things on that level are things that are regarded as mine, for me, as myself.

305
00:21:22,042 --> 00:21:26,326
[SPEAKER_01]: Things that you can exercise even, like, rudimentary levels of control.

306
00:21:26,847 --> 00:21:31,030
[SPEAKER_01]: That sense of control is a necessary basis for a sense of self.

307
00:21:31,150 --> 00:21:33,773
[SPEAKER_01]: It is the sense of self, so to think.

308
00:21:34,413 --> 00:21:43,601
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you go beyond in terms of the discernment of that relationship, that means that neither that and also... well, that means that you will see that, yeah, wholesome is defined by

309
00:21:44,677 --> 00:21:47,539
[SPEAKER_01]: That which is not done in terms of unwholesome.

310
00:21:48,740 --> 00:21:52,202
[SPEAKER_01]: And, consequently, unwholesome is the absence of wholesome.

311
00:21:52,802 --> 00:21:54,964
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is no need for you to hold on to anything.

312
00:21:54,984 --> 00:21:56,324
[SPEAKER_01]: Those things define itself.

313
00:21:56,464 --> 00:21:58,306
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's determinations.

314
00:21:58,326 --> 00:22:05,410
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why ignorance of those... of that... the determination relationships results in appropriation of things.

315
00:22:05,430 --> 00:22:06,691
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where Vijaya is the first.

316
00:22:09,169 --> 00:22:14,352
[SPEAKER_01]: Non-ignorance of that relationship results in dissolvement of ownership of that relationship.

317
00:22:14,412 --> 00:22:16,854
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't own either good or bad, either wholesome or unwholesome.

318
00:22:16,874 --> 00:22:17,534
[SPEAKER_01]: You go beyond it.

319
00:22:21,056 --> 00:22:25,039
[SPEAKER_01]: Because your actions are completely unnecessary, redundant.

320
00:22:25,199 --> 00:22:26,180
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no room for them.

321
00:22:27,280 --> 00:22:28,881
[SPEAKER_01]: Unwholesome is defined by wholesome.

322
00:22:30,162 --> 00:22:31,743
[SPEAKER_01]: Wholesome is the absence of unwholesome.

323
00:22:34,438 --> 00:22:40,963
[SPEAKER_01]: But obviously, for as long as your behavior has been engaging in unwholesome, you've been perpetuating that mistake, maintaining that mistaken view.

324
00:22:41,904 --> 00:22:51,451
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you can see self and not hold even to self... Then, it's automatically not self.

325
00:22:52,352 --> 00:22:54,954
[SPEAKER_00]: But self is still there as something that you don't hold.

326
00:22:55,494 --> 00:22:56,535
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's not yourself then.

327
00:22:56,935 --> 00:22:57,155
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

328
00:22:57,176 --> 00:23:00,518
[SPEAKER_01]: Because self is defined not by what is there.

329
00:23:00,878 --> 00:23:04,161
[SPEAKER_01]: Self is defined by whether what's there is being held as mine or not.

330
00:23:05,782 --> 00:23:11,223
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by seeing self as something there, that means it already can be regarded as not mine.

331
00:23:14,344 --> 00:23:14,944
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you see what I mean?

332
00:23:20,745 --> 00:23:30,366
[SPEAKER_01]: So... That sense of self that would be there, if seen as not self, sorry, as not mine, first it needs to be acknowledged, the sense of self.

333
00:23:30,726 --> 00:23:33,687
[SPEAKER_01]: If you deny there is no self, there is no self, you'll never see that which is yourself.

334
00:23:34,661 --> 00:23:36,523
[SPEAKER_01]: Once you start seeing it, you start seeing it as a phenomenon.

335
00:23:36,563 --> 00:23:38,544
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's a phenomenon, it's something that has a reason.

336
00:23:39,045 --> 00:23:44,770
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's something that has a reason, it cannot be that master behind the arising, behind the manifestation, that controller.

337
00:23:45,170 --> 00:23:45,330
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

338
00:23:45,350 --> 00:23:46,131
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's manifest.

339
00:23:46,171 --> 00:23:48,513
[SPEAKER_01]: It just partakes in the same thing, shares the same nature.

340
00:23:49,974 --> 00:23:50,875
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not mine.

341
00:23:51,415 --> 00:23:52,956
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but that's possible to see only once.

342
00:23:53,076 --> 00:23:56,599
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that is higher than mine.

343
00:23:57,300 --> 00:24:00,623
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in a way, you can use that reflection, like, which is greater, not mine or mine?

344
00:24:01,438 --> 00:24:03,179
[SPEAKER_01]: In general, what is more?

345
00:24:03,680 --> 00:24:05,661
[SPEAKER_01]: Things that don't belong to you or things that belong to you?

346
00:24:05,741 --> 00:24:06,922
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, things that don't belong to me.

347
00:24:06,942 --> 00:24:08,584
[SPEAKER_01]: It's far more numerous in this world.

348
00:24:09,464 --> 00:24:11,005
[SPEAKER_01]: That is greater than things that belong to you.

349
00:24:11,105 --> 00:24:18,631
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you can use simply that discernment on a quite mundane level, in a way, and actually develop your mind on account of it.

350
00:24:18,731 --> 00:24:25,997
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you stick to it, discerning it, you would see that then whatever is yours, it's completely gratuitously, accidentally solved.

351
00:24:27,170 --> 00:24:31,972
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you're not in control of things that are taken to be yours, which means, oh, they're not really mine.

352
00:24:32,733 --> 00:24:37,215
[SPEAKER_01]: They're... basically, things that are yours are really things that are not yours, that are just closer to you.

353
00:24:37,695 --> 00:24:39,296
[SPEAKER_01]: They're things that are really not, not yours.

354
00:24:40,357 --> 00:24:43,178
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not like, oh, this is not mine, and this is mine.

355
00:24:43,298 --> 00:24:47,661
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, this is not my... not, not mine, and this is not mine.

356
00:24:49,561 --> 00:24:51,603
[SPEAKER_01]: But, fundamentally, none of it is yours.

357
00:24:52,463 --> 00:24:53,303
[SPEAKER_01]: All of it is not mine.

358
00:24:54,544 --> 00:24:56,205
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, for an arahant,

359
00:24:59,317 --> 00:25:02,558
[SPEAKER_00]: Not mine is higher than that.

360
00:25:04,039 --> 00:25:08,541
[SPEAKER_00]: Because not mine is opposed to mine.

361
00:25:09,081 --> 00:25:10,862
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's what an Ārya needs to keep doing.

362
00:25:11,102 --> 00:25:14,103
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why he said, not I am, not mine, not myself.

363
00:25:14,183 --> 00:25:15,443
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the reflection of Ārya.

364
00:25:15,623 --> 00:25:16,104
[SPEAKER_01]: He goes back.

365
00:25:16,124 --> 00:25:17,524
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't even need to do that anymore.

366
00:25:17,604 --> 00:25:18,545
[SPEAKER_00]: It ceased completely.

367
00:25:18,605 --> 00:25:19,865
[SPEAKER_01]: Notions of mine and not mine.

368
00:25:20,565 --> 00:25:22,386
[SPEAKER_01]: Notions of wholesome and unwholesome.

369
00:25:22,426 --> 00:25:24,347
[SPEAKER_01]: Notions of good and bad have gone.

370
00:25:24,547 --> 00:25:25,247
[SPEAKER_01]: He went beyond.

371
00:25:25,267 --> 00:25:27,128
[SPEAKER_01]: Eightfold path he went beyond.

372
00:25:30,026 --> 00:25:31,848
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is the highest determination?

373
00:25:34,430 --> 00:25:36,712
[SPEAKER_00]: Determination that he...

374
00:25:37,072 --> 00:25:42,256
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know what I'm trying to say.

375
00:25:42,276 --> 00:25:46,160
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the highest... What is the highest thing he sees?

376
00:25:47,061 --> 00:25:48,242
[SPEAKER_00]: This is determined by that.

377
00:25:48,762 --> 00:25:49,483
[SPEAKER_00]: This is determined.

378
00:25:49,503 --> 00:25:50,223
[SPEAKER_00]: Here's what he sees.

379
00:25:50,303 --> 00:25:50,804
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, it's gone.

380
00:25:51,164 --> 00:25:52,425
[SPEAKER_00]: Determinations have ceased.

381
00:25:52,725 --> 00:25:53,146
[SPEAKER_00]: It's okay.

382
00:25:53,166 --> 00:25:54,087
[SPEAKER_00]: That's okay.

383
00:25:54,267 --> 00:25:55,648
[SPEAKER_00]: Determinations have ceased.

384
00:25:56,343 --> 00:26:01,346
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's determined by all his previous efforts towards ceasing determinations.

385
00:26:01,466 --> 00:26:02,007
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's gone.

386
00:26:02,027 --> 00:26:02,967
[SPEAKER_01]: That's irreversible.

387
00:26:05,929 --> 00:26:09,691
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is nothing dependent on... That's still undetermined.

388
00:26:09,811 --> 00:26:10,252
[SPEAKER_01]: Nibbāna.

389
00:26:11,552 --> 00:26:12,393
[SPEAKER_01]: Asaṅgata, yeah?

390
00:26:12,913 --> 00:26:13,814
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the definition of it.

391
00:26:13,834 --> 00:26:14,254
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

392
00:26:14,714 --> 00:26:16,515
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing dependent on anything anymore.

393
00:26:16,976 --> 00:26:21,899
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing taking his mind anywhere, anyhow, in any manner, any shape or form at all.

394
00:26:22,039 --> 00:26:22,539
[SPEAKER_00]: Gone.

395
00:26:22,559 --> 00:26:23,400
[SPEAKER_00]: Possibility is gone.

396
00:26:24,285 --> 00:26:24,645
[SPEAKER_00]: anymore.

397
00:26:24,945 --> 00:26:25,486
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

398
00:26:25,646 --> 00:26:26,606
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't need to say it.

399
00:26:26,766 --> 00:26:27,827
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

400
00:26:47,435 --> 00:26:48,716
[SPEAKER_00]: And about life and death.

401
00:26:49,816 --> 00:26:51,857
[SPEAKER_00]: So, would you say that

402
00:26:52,573 --> 00:26:54,295
[SPEAKER_00]: There's life, there's activity.

403
00:26:55,376 --> 00:26:56,517
[SPEAKER_00]: But what is higher than that?

404
00:26:57,577 --> 00:26:58,538
[SPEAKER_01]: What is greater than that?

405
00:26:58,578 --> 00:26:59,679
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, death.

406
00:26:59,699 --> 00:27:01,401
[SPEAKER_01]: Death would be more fundamental.

407
00:27:01,561 --> 00:27:06,805
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because if life would be more fundamental, death would just not be manifested.

408
00:27:07,085 --> 00:27:10,889
[SPEAKER_00]: See, but there's something different as, like, say, there's anger, non-anger.

409
00:27:11,229 --> 00:27:16,073
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's different because it kind of involves you in your entirety.

410
00:27:17,387 --> 00:27:19,727
[SPEAKER_01]: Anger, not anger, offers you some perspective.

411
00:27:20,548 --> 00:27:22,748
[SPEAKER_01]: It's kind of... but this is life and not life.

412
00:27:23,368 --> 00:27:24,628
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I can't stop doing life.

413
00:27:25,048 --> 00:27:29,029
[SPEAKER_01]: Life and death are basically on the level of nibbāna and not nibbāna.

414
00:27:35,190 --> 00:27:36,110
[SPEAKER_01]: You're determined.

415
00:27:36,930 --> 00:27:38,291
[SPEAKER_01]: Determined and undetermined, yeah.

416
00:27:41,091 --> 00:27:46,552
[SPEAKER_01]: But in the same manner, when I say, like, the fact... if you recognize that you can die at any moment,

417
00:27:47,402 --> 00:27:58,610
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, your whole life, anything, your past, your future, your hopes, everything, your fears, your desires, everything, everything, your family, everything, it's just... it becomes quite insignificant.

418
00:28:00,632 --> 00:28:01,312
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you don't know.

419
00:28:01,793 --> 00:28:05,195
[SPEAKER_01]: It all goes away and you cannot possibly prevent it in any manner.

420
00:28:05,635 --> 00:28:09,078
[SPEAKER_01]: If you truly contemplate that, you realize how circumstantial

421
00:28:09,596 --> 00:28:16,358
[SPEAKER_01]: Your entire life, there is no greatness of it, or priority of it, or certain personal values.

422
00:28:16,378 --> 00:28:17,798
[SPEAKER_01]: Completely, it could have been otherwise.

423
00:28:18,779 --> 00:28:27,361
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the recognition, like, oh, the one that the Buddha corrected, like, oh, it could not be for me, it will not be for me.

424
00:28:29,281 --> 00:28:33,603
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the Buddha corrected it in saying, it won't be for me, it will not be for me.

425
00:28:34,883 --> 00:28:35,163
[SPEAKER_01]: As in,

426
00:28:35,587 --> 00:28:40,975
[SPEAKER_01]: The greatest of the protugenous views, they would have said, are the ones, it could not, it could have not been for me.

427
00:28:41,756 --> 00:28:44,220
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is kind of, basically, almost surmounts the entire life.

428
00:28:44,340 --> 00:28:51,350
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't quite get the whole relationship thing between the sankhāra and sankhātadhamma, between the determined.

429
00:28:52,497 --> 00:28:53,898
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can abandon a lot.

430
00:28:54,238 --> 00:29:04,123
[SPEAKER_01]: But the one who gets a lesson, he will say it will not, if not for sure, that in the future won't be for him, because there is no room for the ownership in the present, nor in the past, nor in the future.

431
00:29:04,643 --> 00:29:07,244
[SPEAKER_01]: So he corrects that statement just slightly.

432
00:29:08,965 --> 00:29:10,906
[SPEAKER_01]: Like from a first person to a third person.

433
00:29:10,946 --> 00:29:12,767
[SPEAKER_01]: Something I forgot, it's been a long time since I read it.

